Jorge Llamb=EDas wrote:
> On Sun, Oct 10, 2010 at 5:37 PM, Robert LeChevalier <lojbab@lojban.org> w=
rote:
>=20
>>The problem I thought was being discussed was how to identify something a=
s
>>syntactically part of someone else's text.
>=20
>=20
> You seemed to be arguing that that was the case by default, and you
> needed a FAhO to break that default, which is contrary to everything I
> have ever learned about Lojban.
>=20
> Let me put it this way:
>=20
> A:  (something that parses correctly on its own)
> B:  (something that parses correctly on its own)
>=20
> My default assumption is that what A said is one text, and what B said
> is another text. In some special cases, B may be adding something to
> A's text, but normally they are just responding to A's text with their
> own new text.
>=20
> That's the Lojban I have learned starting from your lessons. Is that
> not your current understanding too?

It is my understanding that this is current usage.  You have convinced=20
me that even as early as the draft lessons, we omitted the .i.  I have=20
found examples in TLI Loglan where the same was done.  On the other=20
hand, I distinctly recall the issue coming up in the early days.

JL7 (1988) has a conversation in which each exchange has an explicitly=20
marked as optionally omitted ".i" at the beginning.  Look in the section=20
  "A Very Short Introduction to Lojban Grammar".  It was understood that=20
the continuation ".i" was there even if not explicit (there are other=20
sample conversations in the same newsletter that do not have the=20
explicit ".i", so I suspect that we just stopped teaching it because=20
everyone (until now) understood that exchanges were continuations.

